"""Dataset v2 hand-written topics: beginner, lifestyle (sleep / hydration / recovery / cardio / stretching),
goals (weight loss / muscle gain) and nutrition guidance. Format: (category, intent, [questions], answer).
Answers contain no exact food nutrient values (those come only from USDA data in build_dataset_v2.py).
"""

BEGINNER = [
    ("workout_exercises", "exercise_search", ["I've never worked out before, where do I start?", "Complete beginner, how do I start exercising?", "How should a total beginner start working out?"],
     "Welcome! Start with 2-3 short full-body sessions a week using a few basic movements: a squat (bodyweight or goblet squat), a hip hinge (glute bridge), a push (incline push-ups), a pull (dumbbell or band row) and a core exercise (plank). Do 2 sets of 10-12 easy reps, focus on form, and add a daily walk. Increase gradually over the first month."),
    ("gym_workouts", "gym_workout", ["What should I do on my first day at the gym?", "First time at the gym, what do I do?", "I'm nervous about my first gym session"],
     "Keep day one simple: 5-10 minutes on a bike or treadmill, then 4-5 machines or basic exercises - leg press, chest press, lat pulldown, seated row and a plank - for 2 sets of 10-12 light reps. Ask staff to show you how to adjust the machines. The goal is to learn the movements and leave feeling good, not exhausted."),
    ("workout_exercises", "exercise_search", ["How much weight should a beginner lift?", "How do I choose the right weight?", "What weight should I start with?"],
     "Pick a weight you can lift for the target reps with good form while feeling you could do 2-3 more. If you can't control it, go lighter; if the last reps feel easy, go a little heavier next set. Beginners progress quickly, so start light and add small amounts each week."),
    ("healthy_lifestyle", "general_fitness", ["How often should a beginner work out?", "How many days a week should beginners train?"],
     "Three non-consecutive days a week (for example Monday, Wednesday, Friday) of full-body training is ideal for beginners, plus light activity like walking on other days. This gives every muscle time to recover while you learn the movements."),
    ("workout_exercises", "exercise_search", ["I can't do a single push-up, what should I do?", "How do I get my first push-up?", "Push-ups are too hard for me"],
     "Start with an easier version: wall push-ups, then incline push-ups with your hands on a bench or table, gradually lowering the height. Also practise slow lowering from the top position. Do 2-3 sets 3 times a week, and as you get stronger, move to knee push-ups and then full push-ups."),
    ("workout_exercises", "exercise_search", ["How do I do my first pull-up?", "I can't do a pull-up, how do I build up?", "Pull-up progression for beginners?"],
     "Build up with rows (dumbbell or inverted rows), lat pulldowns, dead hangs from the bar and slow negative pull-ups (jump to the top and lower yourself over 3-5 seconds). A resistance band looped over the bar also helps. Train these 2-3 times a week and you'll usually see progress within a few weeks."),
    ("exercise_safety", "exercise_safety", ["How do I learn proper form?", "How can I check my lifting technique?", "What if my form is bad?"],
     "Start light, follow clear instructions, and film yourself from the side to compare with good examples. Focus on one cue at a time, like keeping your chest up or knees tracking over toes. A session with a qualified coach is a great investment if you're unsure. Never add weight until the movement feels controlled."),
    ("healthy_lifestyle", "general_fitness", ["Is it normal to be very sore after my first workout?", "Why am I so sore after starting the gym?", "I can barely walk after leg day as a beginner"],
     "Yes - delayed onset muscle soreness is common 24-72 hours after a new workout, and it usually eases within a few days. Gentle movement, walking and a light session help. Next time start with less volume. Severe pain, swelling or very dark urine after a workout is not normal and needs medical attention."),
    ("gym_workouts", "gym_workout", ["What gym machines are good for beginners?", "Which machines should beginners use?", "Are machines safe for beginners?"],
     "Machines are a good starting point because they guide the movement. Useful ones are the leg press, chest press, lat pulldown, seated row, shoulder press, leg curl and leg extension. Adjust the seat so joints line up with the pivot point, start light, and move slowly through a comfortable range."),
    ("home_workouts", "home_workout", ["Simple home workout for beginners?", "Easy beginner workout I can do at home", "Beginner workout with no equipment"],
     "Try this circuit 3 times a week: 10 bodyweight squats, 8 incline push-ups (hands on a table or couch), 10 glute bridges, 8 reverse lunges per leg and a 20-second plank. Rest 60-90 seconds and repeat 2-3 rounds. Add reps over time, then move to harder variations."),
    ("healthy_lifestyle", "general_fitness", ["What mistakes do beginners make at the gym?", "Common beginner workout mistakes?"],
     "Common mistakes are doing too much too soon, lifting too heavy with poor form, skipping the warm-up, changing programs constantly, ignoring legs or back, and not eating or sleeping enough. Pick a simple plan, track it, progress gradually and stay consistent for at least 8-12 weeks."),
    ("healthy_lifestyle", "general_fitness", ["I'm overweight and want to start exercising, what should I do?", "Best way to start exercising when you're heavy?"],
     "Start with low-impact activities that are kind to your joints - walking, cycling, swimming or the elliptical - and build up gradually. Add simple strength exercises like chair squats, wall push-ups and band rows 2-3 times a week. Every bit of activity counts. If you have health conditions, check with your doctor before starting."),
    ("cardio_endurance", "cardio", ["Best cardio for beginners?", "What cardio should a beginner do?", "Easy cardio to start with?"],
     "Brisk walking, cycling (stationary or outdoor), the elliptical and swimming are great low-impact options. Start with 15-20 minutes at a pace where you can still talk, 3 times a week, and add 5 minutes every week or two. Once comfortable, you can try run-walk intervals."),
    ("healthy_lifestyle", "general_fitness", ["Do beginners need a workout plan?", "Should I follow a program as a beginner?"],
     "Yes - a simple plan helps you progress instead of guessing each session. A beginner plan of 3 full-body workouts a week with 5-6 basic exercises, 2-3 sets of 8-12 reps and a little more weight or reps each week is all you need. Stick with it for at least 8 weeks."),
    ("healthy_lifestyle", "general_fitness", ["How long until a beginner sees strength gains?", "When will I get stronger as a beginner?"],
     "Beginners often get noticeably stronger within 2-6 weeks, mostly because the nervous system learns to coordinate the movements. Muscle growth becomes more visible after around 8-12 weeks of consistent training, eating enough protein and sleeping well."),
    ("stretching_flexibility", "stretching", ["Stretches for beginners?", "Simple stretching routine for a beginner?"],
     "A simple routine after exercise: hamstring stretch, quad stretch, calf stretch against a wall, hip flexor stretch, chest stretch in a doorway and a child's pose for the back. Hold each for 20-30 seconds at mild tension and breathe slowly - no bouncing."),
    ("muscle_building", "muscle_gain", ["Can beginners build muscle quickly?", "How fast can a beginner gain muscle?"],
     "Beginners build muscle faster than experienced lifters, sometimes called 'newbie gains'. With 3 full-body sessions a week, progressive overload, around 1.6 g of protein per kg of body weight daily and good sleep, you'll typically see clear changes within 2-3 months."),
    ("weight_loss", "weight_loss", ["Beginner tips for losing weight?", "I'm a beginner and want to lose weight, where do I start?"],
     "Start with a small calorie deficit through simple changes - more vegetables and protein, fewer sugary drinks and snacks - plus daily walking and 2-3 beginner strength sessions a week. Aim to lose about 0.5-1 kg per week, sleep 7-9 hours, and focus on habits you can keep."),
    ("fitness_equipment", "equipment", ["What equipment should a beginner buy?", "Best home equipment for beginners?"],
     "A yoga mat, a set of resistance bands and a pair of adjustable dumbbells (or a couple of fixed weights) let you train every muscle group at home. Add a pull-up bar or kettlebell later. Good, supportive shoes matter more than any gadget."),
    ("healthy_lifestyle", "general_fitness", ["Should beginners do cardio or weights?", "What's more important for a beginner, cardio or strength?"],
     "Both. Aim for 2-3 short full-body strength sessions plus 2-3 cardio sessions (like brisk walking or cycling) each week. If time is short, start with strength twice a week and walk daily - you'll improve both strength and fitness."),
    ("exercise_safety", "exercise_safety", ["How do beginners avoid injury?", "Injury prevention tips for new lifters?"],
     "Warm up for 5-10 minutes, start with light weights, learn each movement slowly, increase load gradually, and rest at least a day between hard sessions for the same muscles. Stop if you feel sharp or joint pain, and don't copy advanced routines before you've built a base."),
    ("healthy_lifestyle", "general_fitness", ["I feel embarrassed at the gym as a beginner", "Gym anxiety, what can I do?", "Everyone at the gym looks experienced"],
     "That's very common, and most people are focused on their own workout. Go at quieter times, bring a written plan so you know what to do, start with machines or a corner with dumbbells, and use headphones. Each visit gets easier - you belong there as much as anyone."),
    ("workout_exercises", "exercise_search", ["Is a squat safe for beginners?", "How do beginners learn to squat?"],
     "Yes - squats are a fundamental movement. Start with a bodyweight squat to a box or chair, keeping your chest up, weight across your whole foot and knees tracking over your toes. Then progress to goblet squats with a dumbbell. Only go as deep as you can control with a neutral back."),
    ("workout_exercises", "exercise_search", ["Should beginners do deadlifts?", "Is deadlifting safe for beginners?"],
     "Beginners can deadlift safely if they learn the hip hinge first - try glute bridges, Romanian deadlifts with light dumbbells or a kettlebell deadlift. Keep a neutral back, the weight close to your body, and push through your heels. Progress to a barbell only once the pattern feels solid, ideally with coaching."),
    ("healthy_lifestyle", "general_fitness", ["What should beginners eat when starting to work out?", "Diet tips for gym beginners?"],
     "Keep it simple: build meals around a protein source, vegetables or fruit, whole-grain carbs and some healthy fats. Spread protein across the day, drink water regularly, and have a meal or snack with protein and carbs within a few hours after training. No special supplements are needed to start."),
    ("cardio_endurance", "cardio", ["Can a beginner do HIIT?", "Is HIIT okay for beginners?"],
     "Beginners can try HIIT, but it's best to build a base first with a few weeks of steady cardio. Then start gently - for example 20 seconds of faster effort and 60-90 seconds easy, 5-6 times, once or twice a week. Low-impact choices like a bike reduce stress on the joints."),
]

LIFESTYLE = [
    ("recovery_sleep", "sleep", ["How much sleep do I need?", "How many hours of sleep should I get?", "Is 6 hours of sleep enough?"],
     "Most adults need 7-9 hours of sleep per night. Regularly getting 6 or fewer hours is linked to slower recovery, more hunger, lower mood and poorer training performance. Keep a consistent bedtime and wake time, even on weekends."),
    ("recovery_sleep", "sleep", ["How can I fall asleep faster?", "Tips for better sleep?", "I can't sleep well, what can I do?"],
     "Keep a regular schedule, get daylight in the morning, limit caffeine after early afternoon, avoid large meals and alcohol close to bedtime, and keep your bedroom dark, cool and quiet. Put screens away 30-60 minutes before bed and try a relaxing routine like reading or slow breathing. See a doctor if poor sleep persists."),
    ("recovery_sleep", "sleep", ["Does sleep help muscle growth?", "Why is sleep important for building muscle?"],
     "Yes - during sleep your body repairs muscle tissue and releases hormones involved in recovery and growth. Short sleep is linked to poorer recovery, lower strength and more muscle loss when dieting. Aim for 7-9 hours to get the most from your training."),
    ("recovery_sleep", "sleep", ["Is napping good for recovery?", "Should I nap after working out?"],
     "A short nap of 20-30 minutes can boost alertness and help recovery, especially if you slept poorly. Avoid long naps late in the afternoon because they can make it harder to sleep at night."),
    ("recovery_sleep", "sleep", ["Does exercise help you sleep better?", "Will working out improve my sleep?"],
     "Regular exercise is linked to falling asleep faster and sleeping more deeply. Morning or afternoon activity, especially outdoors in daylight, works well. If very intense evening workouts keep you awake, finish them a bit earlier."),
    ("recovery_sleep", "sleep", ["Is caffeine bad for sleep?", "When should I stop drinking coffee?"],
     "Caffeine can stay in your system for several hours, so it can reduce sleep quality even if you fall asleep. Many people sleep better if they stop caffeine around 6-8 hours before bedtime - roughly early afternoon."),
    ("hydration", "hydration", ["How much water should I drink a day?", "How much water do I need daily?", "Am I drinking enough water?"],
     "Needs vary with body size, climate and activity, but a common guide is roughly 2-3 litres of total fluid a day for adults, including water from food and other drinks. Drink regularly, more when it's hot or you exercise, and use pale yellow urine as a simple check."),
    ("hydration", "hydration", ["How much water should I drink during a workout?", "Should I drink during exercise?", "Hydration during training?"],
     "Start your workout hydrated, then sip water regularly - roughly every 15-20 minutes during longer or hot sessions. For most workouts under an hour, plain water is enough. Afterwards, drink to replace what you lost; weighing yourself before and after a long session shows how much."),
    ("hydration", "hydration", ["Do I need sports drinks?", "Are electrolyte drinks necessary?", "When should I use electrolytes?"],
     "For most workouts under an hour, water is enough. Sports drinks or electrolytes can help during long (60-90+ minute) or very sweaty sessions, especially in heat, by replacing sodium and providing carbohydrates. For everyday drinking they add sugar you don't need."),
    ("hydration", "hydration", ["Does coffee dehydrate you?", "Do tea and coffee count toward water intake?"],
     "Moderate amounts of coffee and tea still count toward your daily fluids - the mild diuretic effect of caffeine doesn't cancel out the water they provide for regular drinkers. Very large amounts of caffeine can cause jitteriness and poor sleep, so keep intake moderate."),
    ("hydration", "hydration", ["Can you drink too much water?", "Is drinking too much water dangerous?"],
     "Yes, although it's rare. Drinking very large amounts quickly - especially during long endurance events without replacing sodium - can dilute blood sodium (hyponatremia), which is dangerous. Drink to thirst, use electrolytes during very long events, and seek medical help for confusion, headache or vomiting."),
    ("hydration", "hydration", ["What are signs of dehydration?", "How do I know if I'm dehydrated?"],
     "Common signs are thirst, dark yellow urine, dry mouth, headache, tiredness, dizziness and muscle cramps. Drink water and rest in a cool place. Severe dizziness, confusion, fainting or very little urine need urgent medical attention."),
    ("recovery_sleep", "recovery", ["How long does it take muscles to recover?", "How many days rest between workouts?", "How long should I rest a muscle group?"],
     "Most muscle groups recover from a hard session in about 48-72 hours, depending on training volume, sleep and nutrition. That's why many plans train each muscle group 2 times a week with a day or two between. Lighter sessions need less recovery."),
    ("recovery_sleep", "recovery", ["How can I recover faster after workouts?", "Best ways to speed up recovery?", "Recovery tips after training?"],
     "The most effective recovery tools are simple: 7-9 hours of sleep, enough total calories and protein, hydration, and sensible training loads. Light activity like walking, mobility work and gentle stretching can help you feel less stiff. Massage and foam rolling may reduce soreness but don't replace sleep and food."),
    ("recovery_sleep", "recovery", ["Is foam rolling useful?", "How do I use a foam roller?", "Does foam rolling help recovery?"],
     "Foam rolling can temporarily reduce muscle tightness and soreness and improve range of motion. Roll slowly over a muscle for 30-60 seconds, pausing on tender spots, but avoid rolling directly on joints, bones or the lower spine. Use it as part of your warm-up or cool-down."),
    ("recovery_sleep", "recovery", ["Do ice baths help recovery?", "Should I take cold showers after the gym?"],
     "Cold water immersion may reduce soreness after intense sessions, but used right after strength training it may slightly blunt muscle growth over time. It's optional - sleep, food and sensible programming matter much more. If you use it, keep it brief and avoid it if you have heart or circulation problems."),
    ("recovery_sleep", "recovery", ["How many rest days do I need per week?", "Do I need rest days?"],
     "Most people benefit from at least 1-2 rest or easy days per week. Rest lets muscles, joints and the nervous system recover so you can train hard again. Light activity like walking on rest days is fine and helps you feel less stiff."),
    ("recovery_sleep", "recovery", ["What is DOMS?", "Why do my muscles hurt days after a workout?"],
     "DOMS (delayed onset muscle soreness) is the muscle soreness that peaks 24-72 hours after unfamiliar or harder exercise. It's normal and fades within a few days. Light movement helps; it gets milder as your body adapts. Sharp pain, swelling or joint pain is different and should be checked."),
    ("recovery_sleep", "recovery", ["Does massage help recovery?", "Is a sports massage worth it?"],
     "Massage can reduce perceived soreness and help you feel relaxed, and many athletes find it useful. It's a nice extra, not a necessity - consistent sleep, nutrition and sensible training loads are the foundation of recovery."),
    ("cardio_endurance", "cardio", ["How much cardio should I do per week?", "How many minutes of cardio a week?"],
     "Aim for 150-300 minutes of moderate cardio (like brisk walking or easy cycling) or 75-150 minutes of vigorous cardio (like running) per week, or a mix. Spread it over several days and build up gradually if you're starting out."),
    ("cardio_endurance", "cardio", ["Is running bad for your knees?", "Will running damage my knees?"],
     "For most healthy people, running doesn't damage the knees and may even support joint health. Problems usually come from increasing distance too quickly, poor recovery or weak hips and legs. Build mileage gradually, include strength training, and see a professional if you have ongoing knee pain."),
    ("cardio_endurance", "cardio", ["What's the best cardio for fat loss?", "Which cardio burns the most fat?"],
     "The best cardio is the one you'll do consistently. Steady cardio and intervals both help when combined with a calorie deficit. Running, cycling, rowing, swimming and brisk walking all work - more total activity across the week matters most."),
    ("cardio_endurance", "cardio", ["Is cycling good cardio?", "Does cycling build fitness?"],
     "Yes - cycling is an excellent low-impact cardio option that builds aerobic fitness and leg endurance. It's gentle on the joints, works well for intervals, and can be done indoors or outdoors. Pair it with some strength training for upper body and bones."),
    ("cardio_endurance", "cardio", ["Is the rowing machine a good workout?", "What does rowing work?"],
     "Rowing is a great full-body cardio workout that uses the legs, back, core and arms. Drive mainly with your legs, then lean back slightly and pull the handle to your lower ribs. Keep your back neutral, and start with short sessions to learn the technique."),
    ("cardio_endurance", "cardio", ["What is zone 2 cardio?", "Why do people talk about zone 2 training?"],
     "Zone 2 is easy, steady cardio where you can still hold a conversation. It builds your aerobic base, improves endurance and is easy to recover from. Many people do most of their weekly cardio at this intensity, plus one shorter, harder session."),
    ("cardio_endurance", "cardio", ["How do I run longer without stopping?", "How do I increase my running distance?"],
     "Slow down - most beginners run too fast. Use run-walk intervals, increase total time by about 10% per week, and keep most runs at a conversational pace. Strength training for the legs and hips and good sleep help too."),
    ("cardio_endurance", "cardio", ["Is jumping rope good exercise?", "Benefits of skipping rope?"],
     "Yes - jumping rope is a time-efficient cardio workout that builds coordination, calf strength and endurance. Start with short intervals like 30 seconds on, 30 seconds off, land softly on the balls of your feet, and use a surface with some give."),
    ("cardio_endurance", "cardio", ["Is the elliptical good cardio?", "Elliptical vs treadmill?"],
     "The elliptical is a solid low-impact cardio option that's easy on the knees and hips. The treadmill (walking or running) is weight-bearing, which is better for bone health but higher impact. Both improve fitness - choose what feels comfortable and you'll use consistently."),
    ("stretching_flexibility", "stretching", ["How long should I hold a stretch?", "How long to hold static stretches?"],
     "Hold static stretches for about 15-30 seconds at mild tension, repeating 2-3 times. Breathe slowly, don't bounce, and never stretch into sharp pain. Stretching regularly - several times a week - is what improves flexibility."),
    ("stretching_flexibility", "stretching", ["How can I become more flexible?", "How do I improve flexibility?", "I'm very stiff, how do I loosen up?"],
     "Stretch the tight areas 3-5 times a week, holding each stretch 15-30 seconds, ideally after exercise when muscles are warm. Add mobility drills and full-range strength exercises like deep squats and Romanian deadlifts. Flexibility improves slowly, so be consistent for several weeks."),
    ("stretching_flexibility", "stretching", ["What is dynamic stretching?", "Dynamic vs static stretching?"],
     "Dynamic stretching uses controlled movement through a range - leg swings, arm circles, walking lunges - and is ideal for warming up. Static stretching holds a position still for 15-30 seconds and is better after workouts or in separate flexibility sessions."),
    ("stretching_flexibility", "stretching", ["Is stretching every day good?", "Can I stretch daily?"],
     "Yes - gentle daily stretching is safe for most people and helps maintain flexibility. Keep it at mild tension, avoid pain, and focus on areas that feel tight, like hips, hamstrings, chest and calves."),
    ("stretching_flexibility", "stretching", ["Stretches for lower back stiffness?", "How do I stretch my lower back?"],
     "Gentle options are child's pose, knees-to-chest, cat-cow and a supine twist, each held or moved slowly for 20-30 seconds. Strengthening the core and glutes also helps. If back pain is severe, spreads down the leg or comes with numbness, see a doctor or physiotherapist."),
    ("stretching_flexibility", "stretching", ["What is a good cool-down?", "How should I cool down after exercise?"],
     "Spend about 5-10 minutes lowering the intensity - easy walking or cycling - then do a few static stretches for the muscles you worked, holding each 15-30 seconds. Drink some water and breathe slowly to help your body settle."),
]

GOALS = [
    ("weight_loss", "weight_loss", ["How do I lose weight?", "What's the best way to lose weight?", "How can I lose weight safely?"],
     "Weight loss comes from a modest calorie deficit kept over time. Eat mostly whole foods with plenty of protein, vegetables and fiber, cut back on sugary drinks and ultra-processed snacks, walk daily and strength train 2-3 times a week to keep muscle. Aim for about 0.5-1 kg per week and prioritise sleep."),
    ("weight_loss", "weight_loss", ["How fast can I safely lose weight?", "How much weight can I lose per week?"],
     "A safe, sustainable rate for most people is about 0.5-1 kg (1-2 lb) per week, or roughly 0.5-1% of body weight. Faster loss usually means more muscle loss, hunger and fatigue, and it's harder to keep off. People with medical conditions should get personalised advice."),
    ("weight_loss", "weight_loss", ["Why am I not losing weight?", "I'm exercising but not losing weight", "My weight loss stopped"],
     "Common reasons are underestimating food intake, liquid calories, weekend overeating, less daily movement than you think, or water retention masking fat loss. Track food honestly for a week or two, keep protein high, walk more, and judge progress over 2-4 weeks using measurements too. If nothing changes, a doctor or dietitian can check for medical causes."),
    ("weight_loss", "weight_loss", ["Is cardio or weights better for weight loss?", "Should I lift or do cardio to lose fat?"],
     "Both help, and the combination works best. Cardio adds calorie burn and heart health; strength training preserves and builds muscle so more of the weight you lose is fat. Diet sets the calorie deficit, so pair either with sensible eating."),
    ("weight_loss", "weight_loss", ["How do I lose belly fat?", "Best way to get rid of stomach fat?"],
     "You can't target belly fat specifically, but it does reduce as overall body fat drops. Keep a modest calorie deficit, eat plenty of protein and fiber, limit sugary drinks and alcohol, strength train, do regular cardio, sleep 7-9 hours and manage stress. Core exercises strengthen the muscles underneath."),
    ("weight_loss", "weight_loss", ["Do I need to count calories to lose weight?", "Can I lose weight without counting calories?"],
     "Counting calories is one useful tool, not a requirement. Many people lose weight by building habits instead: protein and vegetables at every meal, smaller portions of energy-dense foods, fewer sugary drinks, regular meals and more daily movement. Tracking for a few weeks can help you learn portion sizes."),
    ("weight_loss", "weight_loss", ["What should I eat to lose weight?", "Best diet for fat loss?"],
     "The best diet is one you can sustain in a calorie deficit. Build meals around lean protein, vegetables, fruit, whole grains, legumes and healthy fats; drink water instead of sugary drinks; and keep treats occasional. High-protein, high-fiber meals help keep you full."),
    ("weight_loss", "weight_loss", ["Are crash diets effective?", "Should I do a very low calorie diet?"],
     "Crash diets usually cause fast water and muscle loss, strong hunger, low energy and rebound weight gain. A moderate deficit with enough protein is safer and easier to maintain. Very low calorie diets should only be done under medical supervision."),
    ("weight_loss", "weight_loss", ["How do I keep weight off after losing it?", "How do I maintain weight loss?"],
     "Keep the habits that got you there: regular strength training and daily activity, protein-rich meals, mostly whole foods, and regular check-ins like weekly weigh-ins. Move gradually from your deficit back to maintenance calories, and adjust early if the trend creeps up."),
    ("weight_loss", "weight_loss", ["Is walking enough to lose weight?", "Can I lose weight just by walking?"],
     "Walking helps by increasing daily calorie burn and is easy to sustain, but weight loss mainly depends on your overall calorie balance. Combine more walking with sensible eating, and add strength training to keep muscle. Gradually work toward more steps each day."),
    ("muscle_building", "muscle_gain", ["How do I build muscle?", "What's the best way to gain muscle?", "How can I get bigger?"],
     "Train each muscle group about twice a week with 10-20 hard sets per week, mostly in the 6-15 rep range, finishing sets 1-3 reps from failure, and add weight or reps over time. Eat enough protein (around 1.6 g per kg of body weight per day) and a small calorie surplus, and sleep 7-9 hours. Be consistent for months."),
    ("muscle_building", "muscle_gain", ["How much protein do I need to build muscle?", "Protein needs for muscle gain?"],
     "A common evidence-based target is about 1.6 g of protein per kg of body weight per day (up to about 2.2 g/kg), spread over 3-5 meals with roughly 20-40 g each. Get it from foods like eggs, dairy, meat, fish, legumes and tofu, using protein powder if convenient."),
    ("muscle_building", "muscle_gain", ["How do I bulk without getting fat?", "What is a lean bulk?"],
     "A lean bulk uses a small calorie surplus - roughly 200-300 kcal above maintenance - so you gain about 0.25-0.5% of body weight per month while training hard. Keep protein high, eat mostly whole foods, track your weight trend and waist, and adjust intake if you gain too quickly."),
    ("muscle_building", "muscle_gain", ["I'm skinny and can't gain weight, what should I do?", "How do hardgainers build muscle?"],
     "Eat more consistently: add an extra meal or snack, use calorie-dense whole foods like nuts, nut butter, olive oil, dairy, oats and rice, and drink milk or smoothies. Train with progressive overload 3-4 times a week, track your weight weekly and increase food if it isn't rising."),
    ("muscle_building", "muscle_gain", ["How long does it take to build muscle?", "How fast can you gain muscle?"],
     "Beginners can see noticeable muscle growth in about 2-3 months of consistent training and eating. Progress slows with experience. Track strength, photos and measurements, and think in months and years rather than weeks."),
    ("muscle_building", "muscle_gain", ["How do I get bigger arms?", "Best way to grow arms?"],
     "Train biceps and triceps directly 2 times a week with curls and extensions (8-15 reps, 6-10 hard sets per week each), and keep compound lifts like rows, chin-ups, presses and dips that also work the arms. Add weight or reps steadily and eat enough protein and calories."),
    ("muscle_building", "muscle_gain", ["Can I build muscle with bodyweight exercises?", "Can I gain muscle without weights?"],
     "Yes - bodyweight training builds muscle if you push sets close to failure and keep progressing with harder variations (incline to full to decline push-ups, split squats to pistol progressions), more reps, slower tempo or added weight in a backpack."),
    ("muscle_building", "muscle_gain", ["Do I need to eat more to build muscle?", "Should I eat in a surplus to gain muscle?"],
     "A small calorie surplus makes muscle gain easier, especially for experienced lifters. Beginners and people with more body fat can build some muscle even at maintenance or in a small deficit if protein is high and training is consistent."),
    ("muscle_building", "muscle_gain", ["How much muscle can I gain in a month?", "Realistic muscle gain per month?"],
     "Beginners might gain very roughly 0.5-1 kg of muscle per month in the first year with good training and nutrition; experienced lifters gain much more slowly. Weight gain beyond that is usually fat and water, so keep the surplus modest."),
    ("muscle_building", "muscle_gain", ["Should I do cardio while bulking?", "Will cardio kill my gains?"],
     "Moderate cardio won't stop muscle growth and is good for heart health and recovery. Keep most of it easy, separate hard cardio from leg training when possible, and eat a little more to cover the extra energy."),
]

